    <Section title="Transfer Functions"/>
    <SubSection title="What Are Transfer Functions?" id="What">
        <p>Transfer functions are the Laplace domain representation of signal modification in a system where the input = u(t) or U(s), output = y(t) or Y(s), and the transfer function = h(t) or H(s) where "s" indicates a function in the Laplace domain. The transfer function H(s) is defined as the ratio between the output and the input:</p>
        <DisplayEquation equation="H(s) = \\frac{Y(s)}{U(s)}"/>
        <p>Essentially this means that the output is equal to the input multiplied by the transfer function:</p>
        <DisplayEquation equation="Y(s) = H(s)*U(s)"/>
        <Example id="tf_simple" title="Finding System Output from a Block Diagram" solution="True">
            <div>
                <Image src="/signals/simpleTFex.png"><span class="fw-bold">Figure 1.</span> A single-block system with an input and transfer function.</Image>
            </div>
            <div slot="solution">
                <p>Using the relationship <InlineEquation equation="Y(s) = H(s) \cdot U(s)"/>:</p>
                <DisplayEquation equation="Y(s) = \\frac{4}{(s+3)(s+4)}"/>
            </div>
        </Example>

        <p class="mt-3">Another important consideration of transfer functions is that they can be combined and simplified, similar to the components of an electrical circuit. Transfer functions in series are multiplied together, while branches that are in parallel are added or subtracted depending on the summing junction signs.</p>

        <Example id="tf_combined" title="Single Block Representation of Combined Systems" solution="True">
            <div>
                <Image src="/signals/combinedTFex.png"><span class="fw-bold">Figure 2.</span> Multiple transfer function blocks arranged in series and parallel branches.</Image>
            </div>
            <div slot="solution">
                <p>Combining the series cascade and parallel difference gives the overall transfer function:</p>
                <DisplayEquation equation="H(s) = G_{1}G_{2}(G_{4}G_{5}-G_{3})"/>
            </div>
        </Example>

        <p class="mt-3">Try practicing with systems that combine these aspects, and use the table of common Laplace transforms (<a href="/signals/laplace#Facts">here</a>) to convert your frequency-domain outputs back to the time domain.</p>
    </SubSection>

    <SubSection title="Eigenvalues and Transfer Function Poles" id="EigenvaluesAndPoles">
        <p>In linear systems analysis, the roots of the transfer function's denominator polynomial are termed the <strong>poles</strong> of the system. From the perspective of differential equations and linear algebra, these poles are identical to the <strong>eigenvalues</strong> of the system's dynamic state matrix. Eigenvalues and poles govern how a system evolves over time along its principal directions, defining the natural modes of the system independent of the applied input.</p>

        <SubSubSection title="State-Space Characteristic Equation and Dynamic Modes" id="StateSpaceModes">
            <p>Consider a continuous-time linear time-invariant (LTI) system expressed in state-space representation with state vector <InlineEquation equation="\\mathbf{x}(t) \\in \\mathbb{R}^n"/>, input <InlineEquation equation="\\mathbf{u}(t) \\in \\mathbb{R}^m"/>, and output <InlineEquation equation="\\mathbf{y}(t) \\in \\mathbb{R}^p"/>:</p>
            <DisplayEquation equation="\\dot{\\mathbf{x}}(t) = \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{B}\\mathbf{u}(t)"/>
            <DisplayEquation equation="\\mathbf{y}(t) = \\mathbf{C}\\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{D}\\mathbf{u}(t)"/>
            <p>The natural response describes the unforced behavior of the system (<InlineEquation equation="\\mathbf{u}(t) = \\mathbf{0}"/>):</p>
            <DisplayEquation equation="\\dot{\\mathbf{x}}(t) = \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x}(t)"/>
            <p>Assuming an exponential solution mode of the form <InlineEquation equation="\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\mathbf{v}e^{\\lambda t}"/>, where <InlineEquation equation="\\mathbf{v}"/> is a constant non-zero vector and <InlineEquation equation="\\lambda"/> is a scalar rate, substituting this into the unforced state equation yields:</p>
            <DisplayEquation equation="\\lambda \\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t} = \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t} \\implies (\\lambda \\mathbf{I} - \\mathbf{A})\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}"/>
            <p>For non-trivial solutions (<InlineEquation equation="\\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0}"/>), the characteristic matrix <InlineEquation equation="\\lambda \\mathbf{I} - \\mathbf{A}"/> must have a determinant of zero. This yields the <strong>characteristic equation</strong> of the system:</p>
            <DisplayEquation equation="\\det(\\lambda \\mathbf{I} - \\mathbf{A}) = 0"/>
            <p>The <InlineEquation equation="n"/> roots <InlineEquation equation="\\lambda_1, \\lambda_2, \\dots, \\lambda_n"/> of this polynomial are the <strong>eigenvalues</strong> of matrix <InlineEquation equation="\\mathbf{A}"/>. Each eigenvalue <InlineEquation equation="\\lambda_i"/> produces a fundamental characteristic time-domain mode of the form <InlineEquation equation="e^{\\lambda_i t}"/>. These exponential modes serve as the natural basis functions that compose any transient response of the system.</p>
        </SubSubSection>

        <SubSubSection title="Equivalence Between Transfer Function Poles and Eigenvalues" id="PoleEquivalence">
            <p>To see how the state-space eigenvalues directly map to the Laplace transfer function, take the Laplace transform of the state-space equations with zero initial conditions (<InlineEquation equation="\\mathbf{x}(0) = \\mathbf{0}"/>):</p>
            <DisplayEquation equation="s\\mathbf{X}(s) = \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{X}(s) + \\mathbf{B}\\mathbf{U}(s) \\implies (s\\mathbf{I} - \\mathbf{A})\\mathbf{X}(s) = \\mathbf{B}\\mathbf{U}(s)"/>
            <p>Solving for <InlineEquation equation="\\mathbf{X}(s) = (s\\mathbf{I} - \\mathbf{A})^{-1}\\mathbf{B}\\mathbf{U}(s)"/> and substituting into the output equation <InlineEquation equation="\\mathbf{Y}(s) = \\mathbf{C}\\mathbf{X}(s) + \\mathbf{D}\\mathbf{U}(s)"/> gives the transfer function matrix:</p>
            <DisplayEquation equation="\\mathbf{H}(s) = \\frac{\\mathbf{Y}(s)}{\\mathbf{U}(s)} = \\mathbf{C}(s\\mathbf{I} - \\mathbf{A})^{-1}\\mathbf{B} + \\mathbf{D}"/>
            <p>Recall from matrix algebra that the inverse of <InlineEquation equation="s\\mathbf{I} - \\mathbf{A}"/> can be written using its adjugate and determinant:</p>
            <DisplayEquation equation="(s\\mathbf{I} - \\mathbf{A})^{-1} = \\frac{\\text{adj}(s\\mathbf{I} - \\mathbf{A})}{\\det(s\\mathbf{I} - \\mathbf{A})}"/>
            <p>Substituting this relation into the transfer function formula reveals:</p>
            <DisplayEquation equation="\\mathbf{H}(s) = \\frac{\\mathbf{C}\\,\\text{adj}(s\\mathbf{I} - \\mathbf{A})\\mathbf{B} + \\mathbf{D}\\det(s\\mathbf{I} - \\mathbf{A})}{\\det(s\\mathbf{I} - \\mathbf{A})} = \\frac{\\mathbf{N}(s)}{D(s)}"/>
            <p>Notice that the scalar denominator polynomial <InlineEquation equation="D(s)"/> of the transfer function is identically equal to the characteristic polynomial:</p>
            <DisplayEquation equation="D(s) = \\det(s\\mathbf{I} - \\mathbf{A})"/>
            <p>Therefore, setting the denominator to zero to find the transfer function <strong>poles</strong> (<InlineEquation equation="D(s) = 0"/>) is mathematically identical to solving the eigenvalue characteristic equation (<InlineEquation equation="\\det(\\lambda \\mathbf{I} - \\mathbf{A}) = 0"/>):</p>
            <DisplayEquation equation="D(s) = 0 \\implies s = \\lambda_i"/>
            <p>Under minimal realizations (where no state modes are cancelled between numerator and denominator), the <strong>poles of a transfer function are exactly the eigenvalues of the system matrix</strong>.</p>
        </SubSubSection>

        <SubSubSection title="Real vs. Complex Conjugate Poles and Natural Responses" id="RealVsComplex">
            <p>When computing the output response via partial fraction expansion, terms appear in the form <InlineEquation equation="\\frac{r_i}{s - p_i} = \\frac{r_i}{s - \\lambda_i}"/>. Inverse Laplace transforming each partial fraction term yields an exponential time function <InlineEquation equation="r_i e^{\\lambda_i t}"/>. Writing the complex pole/eigenvalue in Cartesian form as <InlineEquation equation="s = \\sigma + j\\omega"/> allows us to classify the natural responses into two primary families:</p>

            <DisplayTable id="pole_response_comparison_table">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th style="width: 25%;">Pole / Eigenvalue Type</th>
                        <th style="width: 25%;">Complex Coordinate</th>
                        <th style="width: 25%;">Natural Response Mode</th>
                        <th style="width: 25%;">Physical Behavior</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>Purely Real Pole (<InlineEquation equation="\\lambda = \\sigma"/>)</strong></td>
                        <td><InlineEquation equation="\\omega = 0"/>, lying on the real axis</td>
                        <td><InlineEquation equation="y(t) \\propto e^{\\sigma t}"/></td>
                        <td>
                            Monotonic exponential decay (<InlineEquation equation="\\sigma < 0"/>) with time constant <InlineEquation equation="\\tau = 1/|\\sigma|"/>, or monotonic exponential divergence (<InlineEquation equation="\\sigma > 0"/>). No oscillation occurs.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>Complex Conjugate Pole Pair (<InlineEquation equation="\\lambda = \\sigma \\pm j\\omega"/>)</strong></td>
                        <td><InlineEquation equation="\\omega \\neq 0"/>, symmetric across the real axis</td>
                        <td><InlineEquation equation="y(t) \\propto e^{\\sigma t} \\cos(\\omega t + \\phi)"/></td>
                        <td>
                            Sinusoidal oscillation at frequency <InlineEquation equation="\\omega"/> enveloped by exponential damping (<InlineEquation equation="\\sigma < 0"/>), exponential growth (<InlineEquation equation="\\sigma > 0"/>), or constant amplitude (<InlineEquation equation="\\sigma = 0"/>).
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </DisplayTable>

            <p class="mt-3">The physical contrast between these two behaviors is fundamental to systems engineering:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>Purely Real Poles (<InlineEquation equation="\\lambda = \\sigma"/>):</strong> 
                    Represent overdamped or first-order dynamics (such as a simple resistor-capacitor <InlineEquation equation="RC"/> low-pass filter or thermal dissipation). The response changes smoothly without ringing or overshoot. 
                    If <InlineEquation equation="\\sigma < 0"/>, the signal decays exponentially toward equilibrium at rate <InlineEquation equation="|\\sigma|"/>. The closer <InlineEquation equation="\\sigma"/> is to the imaginary axis (smaller <InlineEquation equation="|\\sigma|"/>), the slower the decay, making it a <em>dominant pole</em> that dictates settling time. If <InlineEquation equation="\\sigma > 0"/>, the response diverges monotonically to infinity.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Complex Conjugate Pairs (<InlineEquation equation="\\lambda = \\sigma \\pm j\\omega"/>):</strong> 
                    Represent underdamped second-order dynamics where two energy-storage elements exchange energy back and forth (such as mass-spring-damper mechanics or inductor-capacitor <InlineEquation equation="LC"/> tank circuits). 
                    Because physical differential equations have real coefficients, complex poles always appear in conjugate pairs. Combining their exponential terms via Euler's identity produces:
                    <DisplayEquation equation="y_{\\text{pair}}(t) = 2|r| e^{\\sigma t} \\cos(\\omega t + \\angle r)"/>
                    Here, the real part <InlineEquation equation="\\sigma"/> serves as the envelope decay/growth rate, while the imaginary part <InlineEquation equation="\\omega"/> determines the oscillation frequency (ringing rate in rad/s).
                </li>
            </ul>

            <CalloutCard title="Connecting Poles to System Stability">
                <p class="mb-2">Because the real part <InlineEquation equation="\\sigma = \\text{Re}(\\lambda)"/> appears directly in the exponent <InlineEquation equation="e^{\\sigma t}"/>, it strictly dictates whether transients decay to zero (stable, <InlineEquation equation="\\sigma < 0"/>), remain bounded (marginally stable, <InlineEquation equation="\\sigma = 0"/>), or blow up to infinity (unstable, <InlineEquation equation="\\sigma > 0"/>).</p>
                <div class="mt-3">
                    <a href="/signals/stability?origin=coursemenu" class="btn btn-primary d-inline-flex align-items-center gap-2">
                        <i class="bi bi-box-arrow-up-right"></i>
                        <span>Explore Pole-Zero Mapping on the System Stability Page</span>
                    </a>
                </div>
            </CalloutCard>
        </SubSubSection>
    </SubSection>

    <SubSection title="Interactive Transfer Function Lab" id="Simulator">
        <p>Experiment with transfer functions using the interactive simulator below. Choose different system architectures (1st-order lags, 2nd-order resonant systems, series cascades, and parallel branches), test various input waveforms (<InlineEquation equation="u(t)"/> step, <InlineEquation equation="\\delta(t)"/> impulse, pulse, or sinusoid), and observe how transfer function poles, damping ratio <InlineEquation equation="\\zeta"/>, and block algebra directly shape the real-time output signal <InlineEquation equation="y(t)"/>.</p>
        <TransferFunctionSimulator />
    </SubSection>

